# Line endings for this repository.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR. `core.autocrlf` is `input` on at least one machine here, and until this file
# existed that setting was the ONLY thing keeping the index LF. A setting is per-machine and
# unversioned: a clone on a host configured differently commits CRLF, and nothing in the repository
# says otherwise. `* text=auto eol=lf` moves the decision into the repository, where it is reviewed
# with everything else. Prettier already pins `endOfLine: 'lf'` in `prettier.config.js`, which covers
# the files the formatter writes and nothing else -- this covers the rest.
#
# IT COST NOTHING TO ADD, AND THAT WAS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The change was deferred for years
# on the belief that it would renormalise a large part of the working tree. It does not. Measured on
# the commit that added this file, `git ls-files --eol` reports 11 of 2,133 tracked files as `w/crlf`
# and ZERO as `w/mixed`; all 11 are `reference/data/*.csv`, and all 11 already carry `attr/-text`
# from `reference/data/.gitattributes`. Everything else was `i/lf w/lf` before this file and is
# `i/lf w/lf` after it. `git status` stays clean across the change. Re-measure the same way before
# widening any pattern below.
#
# `text=auto` and not a blanket `text`: git keeps its own binary heuristic, so a file it reads as
# binary is left byte-exact; a baseline PNG is `i/-text` and stays untouched. (A plain-ASCII script
# that git scored binary once sat beside them, so re-measure rather than assume.)
* text=auto eol=lf

# THE CARVE-OUT, AND WHY IT IS THE WHOLE MIRROR RATHER THAN THE CSVs.
#
# `reference/` is other people's bytes. Each import's `MANIFEST.sha256` records a sha256 and a byte
# count per file, and `reference/README.md` calls that manifest half the import contract -- so the
# bytes ARE the artifact, and any end-of-line conversion in either direction breaks the digest the
# gate re-derives. `npm run reference:check` would go red on every clone whose git had rewritten the
# file it is verifying, which is worse than no gate at all.
#
# This is not hypothetical for `data/`: those eleven CSVs are UTF-8 with BOM and CRLF, and
# `reference/data/.gitattributes` has said `*.csv -text` since the first two arrived. That nested
# file is the local, load-bearing statement and stays authoritative -- a subdirectory's
# `.gitattributes` outranks this one, so it would win here regardless. The rule is repeated at the
# root so the exemption is visible from the file that creates the need for it.
#
# The earlier skill mirror under `reference/` was LF and needed no conversion, but it was pinned by
# the same kind of manifest and it is the import that actually drifted once: `prettier --write .`
# reformatted `ui-enforcement-policy.json` on the way in and the README went on claiming 6 of 6
# digests reproduced. A mirror-wide `-text` keeps the next byte-exact import
# protected from the moment it lands rather than from the moment somebody remembers to add a pattern.
# (a later decision deleted that mirror on 2026-09-07; `data/` is the one import today, and the rule stays for
# the next.)
reference/** -text
